// **A receiver that does not order is refused at `.compare()` where it is written**, the same
// place and for the same reason `<` refuses it.
//
// `compare` is `Comparable`'s method, so its availability is the trait's membership — a fact the
// checker has for every receiver whose ordering is *decided* here: a built-in kind, a type this
// program declares, and a **native** type, whose traits are its own ABI declaration. Without this
// the method's return came from a built-in table with a row for every receiver kind, so
// `xs.compare(ys)` on a list, a tuple or an unordered native type checked clean and aborted at the
// first call while `xs < ys` on the very same pair was refused where it was written.
//
// `Duration` is the case that needs the native half and the one that shows the two doors are one
// rule: it renders (`Display`) but declares no ordering, because a calendar span has none without
// a reference date — so both `a < b` and `a.compare(b)` must say so statically. Its `<` half is
// `std/datetime_duration_unordered.noe`; this is its method twin.
//
// `Iterator<T>` and `Future<T>` are the third kind: nominal, but the checker's own — no program can
// implement them, so what the checker knows about them is all there is to know.
//
// **Every call below sits in a function nothing calls**, which is what makes the case
// discriminating: an abort raises the same `E0007` at the same span a static rejection does, so a
// position alone would be satisfied by either. Unreachable code has no run-time behavior to
// misread — without the check this file compiles, runs `main`, prints `ok` and exits 0.
//
// The undecided receivers are asserted by their absence from the list: a `dyn` value and an
// unbounded type parameter both reach `.compare()`, because "not known" is not "does not order" —
// a `dyn` may hold an `int`, and a `T` is licensed by its bound elsewhere.
// expect: error E0007 at 40:31
// expect: error E0007 at 41:32
// expect: error E0007 at 42:30
// expect: error E0007 at 43:30
// expect: error E0007 at 44:34
// expect: error E0007 at 49:12
// expect: error E0007 at 52:35
// expect: error E0007 at 54:33
// expect: exit 1
use std.{datetime}

struct Unordered { n: int }

fn lists(): Ordering { return [1].compare([2]) }
fn tuples(): Ordering { return (1, 2).compare((1, 3)) }
fn maps(): Ordering { return {"a": 1}.compare({"b": 1}) }
fn sets(): Ordering { return [1].to_set().compare([2].to_set()) }
fn declared(): Ordering { return Unordered { n: 1 }.compare(Unordered { n: 2 }) }

fn spans(): Ordering {
    t = datetime.from_unix_ms(0)
    a = t.diff(t.add(datetime.hours(1)))
    return a.compare(t.diff(t.add(datetime.hours(2))))
}

fn iterators(): Ordering { return [1].iter().compare([2].iter()) }
async fn one(): int { return 1 }
fn futures(): Ordering { return one().compare(one()) }

// Undecided receivers keep the call: neither of these is refused.
fn through_dyn(x: dyn, y: dyn): Ordering { return x.compare(y) }
fn through_param<T>(x: T, y: T): Ordering { return x.compare(y) }

echo "ok ${through_dyn(1, 2)} ${through_param(1, 2)}"
